# Conservation Renewal Agenda

## v0.7.0 — Water & Watersheds Research Framework

### 1. Purpose

Water is one of the most institutionally fragmented conservation resources in the United States.

Unlike a federal wildlife refuge or public-land unit, a watershed generally has no single owner and no single governing institution. Water moves across property boundaries, political jurisdictions, ecological systems, and administrative authorities.

A river can simultaneously be:

* wildlife habitat;
* fish habitat;
* drinking-water supply;
* irrigation infrastructure;
* hydropower resource;
* transportation corridor;
* flood-management system;
* cultural resource;
* recreation resource;
* municipal infrastructure;
* Tribal resource.

This makes water particularly useful for testing the emerging American Conservation Renewal framework.

The central question for this pillar is:

> **Can American water-governance institutions maintain ecological function while allocating and managing water among competing human and ecological demands across watersheds that rarely correspond to political boundaries?**

