# 25. Research Hypotheses

The water pillar will test the following hypotheses.

### H1

Water governance performs better when ecological boundaries are incorporated into planning.

### H2

Water-quality regulation is more effective when monitoring and implementation are closely linked.

### H3

Water quantity and ecological condition cannot always be managed independently.

### H4

Institutional fragmentation is a major constraint in large watersheds.

### H5

Environmental-flow approaches can improve aquatic outcomes where sufficient authority exists to modify flow regimes.

### H6

Groundwater management requires longer monitoring horizons than many existing planning systems provide.

### H7

Early detection and prevention are particularly important for aquatic invasive species.

### H8

Infrastructure decisions produce better conservation outcomes when ecological effects are incorporated before construction or licensing rather than addressed afterward.

### H9

Tribal water governance and treaty rights are substantive components of watershed governance rather than secondary stakeholder considerations.

### H10

Watershed-scale institutions can improve coordination but do not automatically resolve underlying allocation conflicts.

